1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,233
In this lesson, we'll study arithmetic
sequences, lists of numbers that grow by

2
00:00:05,233 --> 00:00:06,955
the same amount each step.

3
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:13,050
Look at three, seven, eleven, fifteen.
Each term is four more than the one

4
00:00:13,050 --> 00:00:13,519
before.

5
00:00:13,969 --> 00:00:18,513
That fixed step is called the common
difference, d. Here d equals four.

6
00:00:19,633 --> 00:00:25,884
To jump straight to any term, use a sub n
equals a sub one plus n minus one times d.

7
00:00:26,334 --> 00:00:30,537
You start at the first term and add the
difference n minus one times.

8
00:00:31,667 --> 00:00:35,293
Find the fiftieth term of three, seven,
eleven, and so on.

9
00:00:35,743 --> 00:00:41,502
a fifty equals three plus forty nine times
four, which is three plus one ninety six,

10
00:00:41,502 --> 00:00:42,805
or one ninety nine.

11
00:00:43,933 --> 00:00:49,565
Sometimes you know two terms. Suppose a
sub four is ten and a sub nine is thirty.

12
00:00:50,015 --> 00:00:55,816
Five steps take you from ten to thirty, so
d equals twenty over five, which is four.

13
00:00:55,816 --> 00:01:00,789
Then a sub one equals ten minus three
times four, which is negative two.

14
00:01:01,933 --> 00:01:07,371
You can also describe the sequence
recursively: a sub one equals three, and

15
00:01:07,371 --> 00:01:10,851
each a sub n equals the previous term plus
four.

16
00:01:11,301 --> 00:01:16,460
The explicit formula is faster for
far-away terms; the recursive one shows

17
00:01:16,460 --> 00:01:18,063
how each step is built.

18
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:23,531
Your turn. Find the twentieth term of
twelve, nine, six, and so on.

19
00:01:23,981 --> 00:01:25,517
Pause the video and try it.

20
00:01:25,967 --> 00:01:31,782
d is negative three, so a twenty equals
twelve plus nineteen times negative three,

21
00:01:31,782 --> 00:01:33,839
which is negative forty five.

22
00:01:34,966 --> 00:01:40,215
In this lesson, we'll study geometric
sequences, where each term is multiplied

23
00:01:40,215 --> 00:01:41,494
by the same number.

24
00:01:42,633 --> 00:01:48,115
Look at two, six, eighteen, fifty four.
Each term is three times the one before.

25
00:01:48,565 --> 00:01:53,451
That fixed multiplier is the common ratio,
r. Here r equals three.

26
00:01:54,599 --> 00:02:00,530
The nth term is a sub n equals a sub one
times r to the n minus one.

27
00:02:00,980 --> 00:02:04,372
It's an exponential function, sampled at
whole numbers.

28
00:02:05,499 --> 00:02:08,699
Find the eighth term of two, six,
eighteen, and so on.

29
00:02:09,149 --> 00:02:13,961
a eight equals two times three to the
seventh, which is two times two thousand

30
00:02:13,961 --> 00:02:18,280
one hundred eighty seven, or four thousand
three hundred seventy four.

31
00:02:19,399 --> 00:02:22,663
Suppose a sub one is five and a sub four
is forty.

32
00:02:23,113 --> 00:02:29,215
Then five times r cubed equals forty, so r
cubed equals eight and r equals two.

33
00:02:30,333 --> 00:02:33,917
To tell the two kinds apart, check
differences and ratios.

34
00:02:34,367 --> 00:02:40,357
Eighty, forty, twenty, ten has ratio one
half, so it is geometric. Five, eight,

35
00:02:40,357 --> 00:02:44,073
eleven has difference three, so it is
arithmetic.

36
00:02:45,199 --> 00:02:49,999
Your turn. Find the sixth term of eighty
one, twenty seven, nine, and so on.

37
00:02:50,449 --> 00:02:51,985
Pause the video and try it.

38
00:02:52,435 --> 00:02:58,214
r is one third, so a six equals eighty one
times one third to the fifth, which is one

39
00:02:58,214 --> 00:02:58,622
third.

40
00:02:59,766 --> 00:03:05,266
In this lesson, we'll write sums compactly
with sigma notation, and add arithmetic

41
00:03:05,266 --> 00:03:06,273
series quickly.

42
00:03:07,399 --> 00:03:13,116
The Greek letter sigma means sum. Below
it, k equals one tells you where to start;

43
00:03:13,116 --> 00:03:15,207
above it, the last value of k.

44
00:03:15,657 --> 00:03:19,199
The expression to the right tells you what
to add for each k.

45
00:03:20,333 --> 00:03:26,605
The sum from k equals one to four of two k
plus one means three plus five plus seven

46
00:03:26,605 --> 00:03:27,351
plus nine.

47
00:03:27,801 --> 00:03:29,337
That adds to twenty four.

48
00:03:30,466 --> 00:03:36,315
For an arithmetic series, S sub n equals n
over two times the first term plus the

49
00:03:36,315 --> 00:03:37,037
last term.

50
00:03:37,487 --> 00:03:42,404
Pair the first and last, the second and
second to last, and every pair has the

51
00:03:42,404 --> 00:03:43,097
same total.

52
00:03:44,233 --> 00:03:47,731
Add one plus two plus three, all the way
to one hundred.

53
00:03:48,181 --> 00:03:53,640
That's one hundred over two times one plus
one hundred, which is fifty times one

54
00:03:53,640 --> 00:03:56,096
hundred one, or five thousand fifty.

55
00:03:57,233 --> 00:04:01,905
Find the sum from k equals one to thirty
of four k minus one.

56
00:04:02,355 --> 00:04:07,468
The first term is three, the last is one
hundred nineteen. So S equals fifteen

57
00:04:07,468 --> 00:04:12,253
times one hundred twenty two, which is one
thousand eight hundred thirty.

58
00:04:13,399 --> 00:04:18,510
Your turn. Find the sum of the first
twenty terms of five, nine, thirteen, and

59
00:04:18,510 --> 00:04:18,903
so on.

60
00:04:19,353 --> 00:04:20,889
Pause the video and try it.

61
00:04:21,339 --> 00:04:27,221
The twentieth term is five plus nineteen
times four, which is eighty one. S equals

62
00:04:27,221 --> 00:04:30,449
ten times eighty six, or eight hundred
sixty.

63
00:04:31,566 --> 00:04:36,537
In this lesson, we'll add up a finite
geometric series with one formula.

64
00:04:37,666 --> 00:04:44,232
The sum of the first n terms is S sub n
equals a sub one times one minus r to the

65
00:04:44,232 --> 00:04:46,178
n, all over one minus r.

66
00:04:46,628 --> 00:04:49,828
It works for any ratio except r equals
one.

67
00:04:50,966 --> 00:04:54,422
Here's why. Write S, then multiply
everything by r.

68
00:04:54,872 --> 00:05:01,742
Subtract the two lines: almost every term
cancels, leaving S minus r S equals a one

69
00:05:01,742 --> 00:05:05,219
minus a one r to the n. Factor and divide.

70
00:05:06,366 --> 00:05:09,310
Add three plus six plus twelve, for eight
terms.

71
00:05:09,760 --> 00:05:15,461
S eight equals three times one minus two
to the eighth, over one minus two. That's

72
00:05:15,461 --> 00:05:21,231
three times negative two fifty five over
negative one, which is seven hundred sixty

73
00:05:21,231 --> 00:05:21,579
five.

74
00:05:22,699 --> 00:05:28,587
Find the sum from k equals one to six of
four times one half to the k minus one.

75
00:05:29,037 --> 00:05:34,835
First term four, ratio one half, six
terms. S equals four times one minus one

76
00:05:34,835 --> 00:05:40,557
sixty fourth, over one half, which is
sixty three over eight, or seven point

77
00:05:40,557 --> 00:05:41,837
eight seven five.

78
00:05:42,966 --> 00:05:48,070
If you save one dollar on day one and
double it each day, how much after ten

79
00:05:48,070 --> 00:05:48,406
days?

80
00:05:48,856 --> 00:05:54,509
S ten equals one times two to the tenth
minus one, over two minus one, which is

81
00:05:54,509 --> 00:05:56,941
one thousand twenty three dollars.

82
00:05:58,066 --> 00:06:01,949
Your turn. Find two plus ten plus fifty,
for five terms.

83
00:06:02,399 --> 00:06:03,935
Pause the video and try it.

84
00:06:04,385 --> 00:06:10,219
r is five, so S equals two times five to
the fifth minus one, over four, which is

85
00:06:10,219 --> 00:06:12,811
one thousand five hundred sixty two.

86
00:06:13,933 --> 00:06:18,818
In this lesson, we'll add infinitely many
terms and still get a finite answer.

87
00:06:19,966 --> 00:06:23,700
Add one half plus one quarter plus one
eighth, and keep going.

88
00:06:24,150 --> 00:06:29,029
The partial sums are one half, three
quarters, seven eighths, creeping toward

89
00:06:29,029 --> 00:06:30,614
one but never passing it.

90
00:06:31,733 --> 00:06:37,837
When the ratio is between negative one and
one, the sum is S equals a sub one over

91
00:06:37,837 --> 00:06:38,730
one minus r.

92
00:06:39,180 --> 00:06:44,428
Here, one half over one minus one half
equals one, just as the picture showed.

93
00:06:45,566 --> 00:06:51,767
If the absolute value of r is one or more,
the terms don't shrink, and the series has

94
00:06:51,767 --> 00:06:53,737
no sum. We say it diverges.

95
00:06:54,187 --> 00:06:56,747
Two plus four plus eight just keeps
growing.

96
00:06:57,866 --> 00:07:03,717
Repeating decimals are infinite geometric
series. Zero point three three three is

97
00:07:03,717 --> 00:07:07,040
three tenths plus three hundredths, and so
on.

98
00:07:07,490 --> 00:07:12,967
S equals three tenths over one minus one
tenth, which is three tenths over nine

99
00:07:12,967 --> 00:07:14,423
tenths, or one third.

100
00:07:15,566 --> 00:07:20,729
A ball dropped from ten feet bounces back
to sixty percent of its height each time.

101
00:07:21,179 --> 00:07:26,764
The down distances total ten over one
minus point six, which is twenty five. The

102
00:07:26,764 --> 00:07:32,488
up distances total six over point four,
which is fifteen. So it travels forty feet

103
00:07:32,488 --> 00:07:32,976
in all.

104
00:07:34,100 --> 00:07:38,622
Your turn. Find the sum of twelve plus
four plus four thirds, and so on.

105
00:07:39,072 --> 00:07:40,608
Pause the video and try it.

106
00:07:41,058 --> 00:07:45,794
r is one third, so S equals twelve over
two thirds, which is eighteen.

